{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788268013,"nonce":"EptsIRNP1Nr8TJwXNR2/iPtl9cg=","message":"`c718e4a` (site-survey, session 13) carries the read this issue's composition rule will need, and it is the place the \"next 7x7 module can still tile in\" question lands.\n\nThe survey publishes three tiers — footprint (the game's gate), interaction cell (standable, exclusive against other interaction cells per `PlaceWorker_PreventInteractionSpotOverlap`, tolerates a chair), and **margin**, which is not a game gate but computed facts over a rect 2–3x the footprint: reachability of the interaction cell, standable ring, adjacent doors. The margin tier is where module phase belongs once this issue lands: \"is the origin on phase\" and \"are the neighbouring module cells still clear\" are two more facts in the same list, computed from the grid this issue defines. Nothing on `c718e4a` blocks on you; when you define the module origin/phase, add the fields there rather than inventing a second survey.\n\nOne convention you both need pinned: `find-rect`'s `at` is a corner, the placement verbs take `GenAdj.OccupiedRect`'s centre, and for even sizes `CellRect.CenterCell` is one cell off from it. A grid with a 7x7 module is odd on both axes, which sidesteps the even-size ambiguity for whole modules — but halves (7x3) and quarters (3x3) do not all stay odd. The survey publishes the game's `pos`; the module grid should be defined in corners (origins), which is what `place-layout --origin` already says.","files":null}]}